NEXUS-RAG GENERALIZATION EVALUATION SET
Version 1.0 — Corpus-Grounded, Audit-Verified
================================================================================

PURPOSE
────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
This is the GENERALIZATION EVAL — distinct from the 55-question REGRESSION SET.
It tests whether Nexus RAG generalizes across:
  A. Paraphrased Questions         (10 questions)
  B. Adversarial Wording           (10 questions)
  C. Unseen Combinations           (10 questions)
  D. Longer User-Style Questions   (10 questions)
  E. Ambiguous Questions           (8 questions)
  F. Out-of-Scope Traps            (8 questions)
  G. Multi-Hop Missing Evidence    (9 questions)

TOTAL: 65 questions

Each question tagged with:
  [DOCS]       — Exact filenames required to answer
  [ANSWER_KEY] — Core elements expected in answer
  [TRAP]       — What the question is designed to stress-test
  [FAIL_MODE]  — What a failing system would do

================================================================================
A. PARAPHRASED QUESTIONS (10)
================================================================================
Same core concept as regression set — different surface form.
Tests: Lexical independence of retrieval, semantic robustness.

──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
PA-01
Question: "What makes the transformer architecture faster to train than LSTMs?"
[DOCS]: transformer_architecture.txt
[ANSWER_KEY]: No recurrent units; all tokens processed in parallel; no sequential dependency
[TRAP]: Paraphrase of "why transformers replaced RNNs" — must retrieve on meaning not keyword
[FAIL_MODE]: Retrieves LSTM content or gives generic answer without parallel processing mechanism

PA-02
Question: "How does a language model learn to follow human preferences without hand-crafted reward functions?"
[DOCS]: reinforcement_learning_with_human_feedback.txt
[ANSWER_KEY]: Reward model trained from human comparison data; PPO optimizes policy against reward model
[TRAP]: Paraphrase of "what is RLHF" — no keyword match on "RLHF" or "reinforcement learning"
[FAIL_MODE]: Returns generic RL content or misses the human feedback mechanism

PA-03
Question: "Why do large models suddenly gain new skills that smaller models lack?"
[DOCS]: large_language_models.txt
[ANSWER_KEY]: Emergent abilities; capabilities arising from scale and complex component interactions; not explicitly programmed
