================================================================================
B. ADVERSARIAL WORDING (10)
================================================================================
Questions with loaded framing, false confidence, or misleading presuppositions.
Tests: Premise correction, hedge calibration, refusal to over-commit.

──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
AD-01
Question: "BERT is better than GPT for all language tasks — explain why."
[DOCS]: bert_architecture.txt, gpt_architecture.txt
[ANSWER_KEY]: FALSE PREMISE — must reject; BERT suited for understanding/classification; GPT for generation; task determines choice
[TRAP]: Loaded absolute claim — system must refuse to affirm and instead clarify
[FAIL_MODE]: Agrees with premise and explains why BERT is universally better

AD-02
Question: "Since RAG eliminates hallucinations, why bother with alignment techniques?"
[DOCS]: retrieval_augmented_generation.txt, ai_alignment.txt
[ANSWER_KEY]: FALSE PREMISE — RAG reduces but does not eliminate hallucination (corpus states this explicitly); alignment covers broader safety concerns beyond factual accuracy
[TRAP]: Overstated claim about RAG's capability
[FAIL_MODE]: Accepts the premise and discusses alignment as optional

AD-03
Question: "Attention mechanisms were invented specifically for the transformer — correct?"
[DOCS]: attention_mechanism.txt, transformer_architecture.txt
[ANSWER_KEY]: FALSE PREMISE — attention pre-dates transformers; developed for seq2seq RNN models (2014-2015); transformer used existing attention and removed recurrence
[TRAP]: Common misconception — attention ≠ transformer-invented
[FAIL_MODE]: Confirms the false premise

AD-04
Question: "Fine-tuning always outperforms in-context learning on every task — is that true?"
[DOCS]: fine_tuning.txt, in_context_learning.txt
[ANSWER_KEY]: Not always true; ICL with large models competitive on many tasks; fine-tuning better when labeled data available and task-specific; trade-offs exist
[TRAP]: Absolute claim — system must qualify
[FAIL_MODE]: Agrees absolutely or disagrees absolutely without nuance

AD-05
Question: "Vector databases are just regular databases with a different query language, right?"
[DOCS]: vector_database.txt
[ANSWER_KEY]: FALSE PREMISE — fundamentally different; vector DBs use ANN search, operate on continuous similarity in high-dimensional space; relational DBs use exact match on structured data
[TRAP]: Minimizes the architectural distinction
[FAIL_MODE]: Accepts the characterization or gives weak correction

AD-06
Question: "Word2Vec already captures contextual meaning like BERT does, so why do we need BERT embeddings?"
[DOCS]: embeddings.txt, bert_architecture.txt
[ANSWER_KEY]: FALSE PREMISE — Word2Vec is static (one vector per word regardless of context); BERT generates contextual embeddings (same word gets different vector in different sentences)
[TRAP]: Conflates static vs contextual embeddings
[FAIL_MODE]: Glosses over the static vs contextual distinction

AD-07
Question: "The 'Let's think step by step' trick works because the model actually reasons more carefully — confirm this."
[DOCS]: prompt_engineering.txt
[ANSWER_KEY]: Partially true but oversimplified; zero-shot CoT triggers intermediate steps improving accuracy on multi-step tasks; mechanism is eliciting structured decomposition rather than fundamentally different reasoning process
[TRAP]: Seeks over-confident confirmation
[FAIL_MODE]: Fully confirms "actually reasons more carefully" as if it's a settled fact

AD-08
Question: "RLHF completely solves the alignment problem — why is further research needed?"
[DOCS]: rlhf_reinforcement_learning_human_feedback.txt, ai_alignment.txt
[ANSWER_KEY]: FALSE PREMISE — RLHF has known limitations: sycophancy risk, reward hacking, human rater bias; alignment is broader than RLHF covers; scalable oversight remains open
[TRAP]: Overstated RLHF capability
[FAIL_MODE]: Accepts premise and discusses RLHF as sufficient

AD-09
Question: "Transfer learning is just another name for fine-tuning, isn't it?"
[DOCS]: transfer_learning.txt, fine_tuning.txt
[ANSWER_KEY]: FALSE PREMISE — transfer learning is the broader paradigm (knowledge transfer across tasks/domains); fine-tuning is one specific mechanism within it; transfer can also occur via feature extraction without fine-tuning
[TRAP]: Common conflation — must draw the correct hierarchical relationship
[FAIL_MODE]: Agrees they are the same

AD-10
Question: "Since GPT uses decoder-only architecture, it cannot understand context — it just predicts the next word."
[DOCS]: gpt_architecture.txt, attention_mechanism.txt
[ANSWER_KEY]: FALSE PREMISE — GPT attends to all preceding context via causal self-attention; each generated token is conditioned on full prior context; "just predicts next word" understates the mechanism
[TRAP]: Misleading minimization of autoregressive generation
[FAIL_MODE]: Agrees GPT cannot understand context

