C. UNSEEN COMBINATIONS (10)
================================================================================
Questions pairing document clusters that were never paired in regression set.
Tests: Compositional retrieval, novel cross-document reasoning.

──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
UC-01
Question: "How does the seq2seq bottleneck problem motivate the use of attention, and how does that same attention mechanism now power semantic search?"
[DOCS]: encoder_decoder_models.txt, attention_mechanism.txt, semantic_search.txt
[ANSWER_KEY]: Seq2seq fixed-size bottleneck → attention allows dynamic focus on relevant tokens → transformer attention generalizes to query-document similarity in semantic search
[TRAP]: Requires three-document chain; no single document has the full answer
[FAIL_MODE]: Answers only seq2seq→attention or only attention→search, not the full chain

UC-02
Question: "What role does negative transfer play in choosing between full fine-tuning and parameter-efficient methods like LoRA?"
[DOCS]: transfer_learning.txt, fine_tuning.txt
[ANSWER_KEY]: Negative transfer occurs when source and target are dissimilar; PEFT methods like LoRA fine-tune fewer parameters, reducing the risk of overwriting general knowledge
[TRAP]: Connects transfer learning theory to PEFT practice — not directly linked in either document
[FAIL_MODE]: Discusses PEFT without connecting to negative transfer risk

UC-03
Question: "How does the concept of power-seeking in AI alignment relate to why we care about the reward model quality in RLHF?"
[DOCS]: ai_alignment.txt, rlhf_reinforcement_learning_human_feedback.txt
[ANSWER_KEY]: Power-seeking emerges from instrumental convergence — agents optimize subgoals regardless of intended goal; poor reward models produce misspecified objectives; RLHF reward quality determines whether policy optimization leads toward or away from power-seeking behavior
[TRAP]: Links alignment theory to RLHF engineering — requires reasoning across both
[FAIL_MODE]: Discusses reward model quality without the power-seeking risk angle

UC-04
Question: "If GPT-3 demonstrated few-shot learning through in-context learning, why do we still need vector databases in RAG systems?"
[DOCS]: gpt_architecture.txt, in_context_learning.txt, retrieval_augmented_generation.txt, vector_database.txt
[ANSWER_KEY]: ICL limited by context window; cannot store entire knowledge base in prompt; vector databases enable retrieval from unlimited corpus; RAG provides current, domain-specific knowledge not in training data
[TRAP]: Pits ICL against RAG — requires understanding complementary roles
[FAIL_MODE]: Treats ICL and RAG as mutually exclusive alternatives

UC-05
Question: "How does the bidirectional attention in BERT affect its suitability as an embedding model for semantic search?"
[DOCS]: bert_architecture.txt, attention_mechanism.txt, semantic_search.txt
[ANSWER_KEY]: Bidirectional attention means BERT reads full context in both directions; produces rich contextual embeddings capturing surrounding meaning; makes BERT-derived embeddings effective for semantic similarity in search
[TRAP]: Connects architectural property (bidirectional) to downstream application (semantic search)
[FAIL_MODE]: Describes BERT's architecture without connecting to search utility

UC-06
Question: "In what way does the pre-LN convention in transformer training relate to the stability challenges seen in RLHF policy optimization?"
[DOCS]: transformer_architecture.txt, rlhf_reinforcement_learning_human_feedback.txt
[ANSWER_KEY]: Pre-LN normalizes before sublayers, stabilizing gradient flow; PPO in RLHF also requires careful stability — KL divergence penalty prevents policy from drifting too far; both address training instability through architectural/algorithmic constraints
[TRAP]: Novel pairing — architectural stability + RL training stability; both grounded in corpus
[FAIL_MODE]: Describes pre-LN or PPO in isolation without connecting the stability theme

UC-07
Question: "How does the concept of meta-learning in in-context learning connect to the broader transfer learning paradigm?"
[DOCS]: in_context_learning.txt, transfer_learning.txt
[ANSWER_KEY]: ICL viewed as meta-learning — model learns to learn from examples; transfer learning transfers knowledge across tasks through weights; ICL is inference-time transfer without weight updates; both are instances of knowledge reuse across task boundaries
[TRAP]: Conceptual bridge between ICL meta-learning and transfer learning theory
[FAIL_MODE]: Describes each separately without connecting the knowledge-reuse principle

UC-08
Question: "What does the quantization of large language models have in common with product quantization in vector databases?"
[DOCS]: large_language_models.txt, vector_database.txt
[ANSWER_KEY]: Both reduce precision to save memory/compute; LLM quantization reduces weight precision (float32→int8); product quantization compresses embedding vectors; both trade slight accuracy loss for significant efficiency gains
[TRAP]: Same concept (quantization) appearing in two very different contexts in the corpus
[FAIL_MODE]: Treats them as unrelated or confuses the two types

UC-09
Question: "How does catastrophic forgetting in neural networks motivate the design of RAG as an alternative to continual fine-tuning for knowledge updates?"
[DOCS]: transfer_learning.txt, fine_tuning.txt, retrieval_augmented_generation.txt
[ANSWER_KEY]: Fine-tuning on new data risks catastrophic forgetting of prior knowledge; RAG avoids this by keeping model weights frozen and updating only the retrieval corpus; knowledge updates without touching model parameters
[TRAP]: Three-document chain connecting forgetting risk to RAG design motivation
[FAIL_MODE]: Describes RAG benefits without connecting to the forgetting problem

UC-10
Question: "How does the attention score computation relate to the similarity metrics used in vector databases?"
[DOCS]: attention_mechanism.txt, vector_database.txt
[ANSWER_KEY]: Attention uses scaled dot product between query and key vectors — mathematically equivalent to dot product similarity; vector databases use cosine/dot product/Euclidean similarity; both score relevance via geometric relationships in vector space
[TRAP]: Mathematical equivalence between attention and vector similarity — elegant cross-document insight
[FAIL_MODE]: Describes each separately without the mathematical connection

