D. LONGER USER-STYLE QUESTIONS (10)
================================================================================
Verbose, colloquial, multi-clause questions as real users write them.
Tests: Query understanding, relevant extraction, ignoring noise.

──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
LU-01
Question: "I'm trying to understand why my team keeps saying we should use RAG instead of just fine-tuning our model on our company's internal documents — can you explain what the actual practical differences are between these two approaches and when you'd pick one over the other?"
[DOCS]: retrieval_augmented_generation_rag.txt, fine_tuning.txt
[ANSWER_KEY]: RAG: dynamic updates without retraining, grounds in current docs, no catastrophic forgetting risk; Fine-tuning: deeper behavioral adaptation, faster inference, works offline; RAG preferred for frequently changing knowledge; fine-tuning for consistent style/behavior
[TRAP]: Verbose phrasing with conversational noise — must extract the core comparison
[FAIL_MODE]: Gets distracted by conversational framing or gives only one side

LU-02
Question: "I've heard that BERT and GPT are both transformers, so why can't I just use BERT to generate text the same way GPT does? Is there something fundamentally different about how they work internally?"
[DOCS]: bert_architecture.txt, gpt_architecture.txt, transformer_architecture.txt
[ANSWER_KEY]: BERT encoder-only with bidirectional attention — sees full sequence simultaneously, cannot autoregressively generate; GPT decoder-only with causal masking — generates one token at a time conditioned on prior tokens; architecturally incompatible generation methods
[TRAP]: Multi-clause question — must answer both "why can't BERT generate" and "what's different internally"
[FAIL_MODE]: Gives only high-level "BERT is for understanding, GPT is for generation" without the architectural explanation

LU-03
Question: "Our RAG system seems to work well for most questions but when users ask very niche domain-specific things that use technical jargon, it returns irrelevant documents — what could be causing this and how would the underlying retrieval mechanism contribute to this problem?"
[DOCS]: semantic_search.txt, embeddings.txt, vector_database.txt
[ANSWER_KEY]: Dense embeddings may not capture rare/domain-specific terms well; embedding models trained on general corpora may not encode niche vocabulary accurately; hybrid retrieval (BM25 + dense) addresses rare term retrieval; domain-specific embedding fine-tuning
[TRAP]: Diagnostic question in user voice — must connect vocabulary gap to embedding limitation to hybrid solution
[FAIL_MODE]: Generic "try better embeddings" without explaining why dense retrieval fails on jargon

LU-04
Question: "Someone told me that with enough prompt engineering you basically don't need to do any fine-tuning at all anymore, but I feel like that can't be right — can you help me understand the real boundaries of what prompt engineering can and can't do compared to actually fine-tuning a model?"
[DOCS]: prompt_engineering.txt, fine_tuning.txt
[ANSWER_KEY]: Prompt engineering: flexible, no training, model-dependent, limited by context window and model's internalized knowledge; fine-tuning: deeper behavioral change, consistent style adaptation, domain-specific vocabulary encoding, not dependent on prompt quality; soft prompting is a middle ground
[TRAP]: Balanced position question — must give honest nuanced answer rather than validating either extreme
[FAIL_MODE]: Sides definitively with one approach or gives symmetric false balance

LU-05
Question: "I keep seeing papers talk about 'emergent capabilities' in large models — can you explain what that actually means technically, give me a concrete example, and tell me why this makes alignment harder?"
[DOCS]: large_language_models.txt, ai_alignment.txt
[ANSWER_KEY]: Emergent abilities: capabilities appearing at scale not present in smaller models; concrete example: in-context learning appeared in GPT-3; alignment harder because emergent behaviors cannot be anticipated during training or evaluation
[TRAP]: Multi-part question — definition + example + alignment implication; all three must be answered
[FAIL_MODE]: Answers definition and example but misses the alignment difficulty connection

LU-06
Question: "I'm building a search engine for scientific papers and I want it to understand what users mean rather than just match keywords — can you walk me through what components I'd need and how they fit together?"
[DOCS]: semantic_search.txt, embeddings.txt, vector_database.txt, retrieval_augmented_generation.txt
[ANSWER_KEY]: Embedding model converts papers + queries to vectors; vector database stores and indexes paper embeddings; semantic search matches query embedding to closest paper embeddings; optionally: reranking with cross-encoder; RAG to generate summarized answers
[TRAP]: Applied system design question — must compose components from multiple documents coherently
[FAIL_MODE]: Describes components in isolation without the integration architecture

LU-07
Question: "What exactly is happening mathematically when a transformer uses attention — I want to understand the actual computation, not just the intuition about 'focusing on relevant words'"
[DOCS]: attention_mechanism.txt, transformer_architecture.txt
[ANSWER_KEY]: Query, key, value matrices derived from input; dot product of Q and K gives scores; scaled by sqrt(d_k) for gradient stability; softmax normalizes to attention weights; weighted sum of V vectors produces output; Attention(Q,K,V) = softmax(QK^T/sqrt(d_k))V
[TRAP]: User explicitly rejects intuition and asks for mechanics — must provide mathematical detail
[FAIL_MODE]: Gives only the intuition the user explicitly said they already have

LU-08
Question: "My manager wants me to deploy our LLM system to production next month but I'm worried about safety — what specific techniques or measures does the field actually recommend before putting something like this in front of real users?"
[DOCS]: ai_alignment.txt, reinforcement_learning_with_human_feedback.txt, prompt_engineering.txt
[ANSWER_KEY]: RLHF alignment before deployment; red teaming; prompt injection defenses; monitoring for reward hacking and sycophancy; human oversight for high-stakes decisions; constitutional AI considerations; output filtering
[TRAP]: Practical deployment framing — must give concrete actionable measures from corpus
[FAIL_MODE]: Generic safety advice not grounded in corpus concepts

LU-09
Question: "I've seen the term 'pre-training' used in totally different ways — sometimes it means training on a huge dataset before fine-tuning, and other times it seems to mean something else — can you clarify what it means in the context of language models specifically?"
[DOCS]: large_language_models.txt, transfer_learning.txt, bert_architecture.txt
[ANSWER_KEY]: In LLMs: self-supervised training on large text corpus using masked LM or autoregressive objective; learns general language representations; contrasted with fine-tuning on task-specific data; the "pre" in pre-training means prior to task-specific adaptation
[TRAP]: Disambiguation of overloaded term — must give LLM-specific definition while acknowledging broader usage
[FAIL_MODE]: Gives only one definition without addressing the user's confusion about multiple uses

LU-10
Question: "Can you explain why training a reward model is so central to RLHF and what could go wrong if the reward model is poorly trained or biased?"
[DOCS]: reinforcement_learning_with_human_feedback.txt, ai_alignment.txt
[ANSWER_KEY]: Reward model is the proxy for human preferences; policy optimized against it; if biased: sycophancy (rewards flattery); reward hacking (policy finds loopholes); Goodhart's Law — optimizing proxy diverges from true goal; biased raters produce biased reward model
[TRAP]: Two-part question: centrality + failure modes; must address both
[FAIL_MODE]: Explains reward model training without the failure mode analysis

