E. AMBIGUOUS QUESTIONS (8)
================================================================================
Questions where the answer depends on interpretation or context.
Tests: Clarification ability, scope awareness, avoiding overcommitment.

──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
AM-01
Question: "What is the best embedding model?"
[DOCS]: embeddings.txt, semantic_search.txt
[ANSWER_KEY]: Must acknowledge ambiguity — "best" depends on task (classification vs search vs generation); corpus discusses tradeoffs between static (Word2Vec) and contextual (BERT); no single best; should ask for use case or present tradeoffs
[TRAP]: Undefined "best" — system must not pick one arbitrarily
[FAIL_MODE]: Confidently names a single embedding model as best without qualification

AM-02
Question: "Is in-context learning the same as few-shot learning?"
[DOCS]: in_context_learning.txt, prompt_engineering.txt
[ANSWER_KEY]: Closely related but not identical; few-shot is providing k examples in prompt; ICL is the broader capability of learning from context without weight updates; few-shot prompting is the primary mechanism that triggers ICL
[TRAP]: Common conflation — must draw precise distinction without overclaiming
[FAIL_MODE]: Says "yes, same thing" or "completely different" — both wrong

AM-03
Question: "Does RAG use fine-tuning?"
[DOCS]: retrieval_augmented_generation.txt, fine_tuning.txt
[ANSWER_KEY]: Ambiguous — basic RAG keeps model frozen; advanced RAG variants may fine-tune retriever or generator; corpus describes both; should clarify which RAG variant is meant
[TRAP]: Question seems simple but has a conditional answer depending on RAG variant
[FAIL_MODE]: Says definitively "yes" or "no" without qualifying the variant

AM-04
Question: "What is context in the context of language models?"
[DOCS]: in_context_learning.txt, retrieval_augmented_generation.txt, transformer_architecture.txt
[ANSWER_KEY]: Must distinguish: (1) context window — tokens the model processes at once; (2) context in ICL — examples provided in prompt; (3) retrieved context in RAG — documents added to prompt; all are valid usages
[TRAP]: Self-referentially ambiguous question — "context" has three distinct meanings across the corpus
[FAIL_MODE]: Gives only one meaning without acknowledging the others

AM-05
Question: "How much data do you need for fine-tuning?"
[DOCS]: fine_tuning.txt, transfer_learning.txt
[ANSWER_KEY]: Depends on: task complexity, domain distance from pretraining, model size, PEFT vs full fine-tuning; BERT established that relatively few examples suffice for many tasks; no single number; must frame as a tradeoff
[TRAP]: Seeks a number — must explain why no universal answer exists while providing useful guidance
[FAIL_MODE]: Names a specific number confidently without qualification

AM-06
Question: "Is attention the same in all transformers?"
[DOCS]: attention_mechanism.txt , transformer_architecture.txt, bert_architecture.txt, gpt_architecture.txt
[ANSWER_KEY]: Core mechanism same (Q, K, V, scaled dot-product); differs in: masking (bidirectional vs causal), cross-attention presence (encoder-decoder only), number of heads, head dimensions, efficient variants (MQA, GQA, MLA)
[TRAP]: Simple-sounding question with a nuanced answer — must identify what varies vs what is invariant
[FAIL_MODE]: Says "yes all the same" or lists differences without anchoring what's common

AM-07
Question: "What is grounding in RAG?"
[DOCS]: retrieval_augmented_generation.txt, ai_alignment.txt
[ANSWER_KEY]: In RAG: grounding means generation is anchored to retrieved source documents, reducing hallucination; in alignment: grounding relates to truthfulness; must scope to RAG context while noting overlap
[TRAP]: "Grounding" used in both RAG and alignment literature with related but different emphasis
[FAIL_MODE]: Conflates the two meanings or answers only one without noting ambiguity

AM-08
Question: "Should I use a smaller or larger chunk size in my RAG system?"
[DOCS]: retrieval_augmented_generation.txt, vector_database.txt
[ANSWER_KEY]: Depends on use case; smaller chunks: precise retrieval, less context per chunk; larger chunks: more context, less precise retrieval, may hit context limits; corpus frames this as a tradeoff not a universal answer
[TRAP]: Practical question with no universal answer — system must frame the tradeoff
[FAIL_MODE]: Recommends a specific size without explaining the tradeoff

